\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces E1 - E5 using five methods. The outcome column gives how each run ended and, in parentheses, the iteration at which it did: \emph  {converged} on the method's own test, \emph  {stalled} on a line-search failure, or \emph  {budget} on the iteration cap. Iteration counts are not comparable across methods, so we measure the runtime.``area error'' measures the largest relative area error and ``centorid error'' is $\qopname  \relax m{max}_i\|\mathbf  {b}_i-\mathbf  {c}_i\|$, both measured on the diagram each method holds when it stops. We dilated FW and SG dil with $t=1250$ for E1--E4 and $t=100$ for E5, consistent with the setting in their paper.\relax }}{8}{table.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{tab:results}{{1}{8}{E1 - E5 using five methods. The outcome column gives how each run ended and, in parentheses, the iteration at which it did: \emph {converged} on the method's own test, \emph {stalled} on a line-search failure, or \emph {budget} on the iteration cap. Iteration counts are not comparable across methods, so we measure the runtime.``area error'' measures the largest relative area error and ``centorid error'' is $\max _i\|\mathbf {b}_i-\mathbf {c}_i\|$, both measured on the diagram each method holds when it stops. We dilated FW and SG dil with $t=1250$ for E1--E4 and $t=100$ for E5, consistent with the setting in their paper.\relax }{table.caption.29}{}}
